WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.080
When "Jeopardy" co-hosts Mayim Bialik&nbsp;
and Ken Jennings were tapped to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.580
over for the late Alex Trebek, some longtime&nbsp;
viewers were none too pleased — and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.590
offensive comments from both co-hosts&nbsp;
didn't exactly win them any fans.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:17.460
Mayim Bialik has been a fixture on television for&nbsp;
as far back as many viewers can remember. Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.200
in the '90s, she starred in the titular role&nbsp;
of Blossom Russo in the NBC sitcom "Blossom"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.000
before pivoting to voice work on cartoons like&nbsp;
"Johnny Bravo," "Recess," and "Kim Possible."

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.260
Today, however, she's better known for her&nbsp;
long-running role as Amy Farrah Fowler on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.740
"The Big Bang Theory," which she played&nbsp;
beginning in Season 3 of the hit sitcom.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.600
More recently, viewers will recognize her from&nbsp;
another sitcom, "Call Me Kat," and as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.980
two recurring hosts on "Jeopardy" following&nbsp;
the passing of longtime host Alex Trebek.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.480
Unfortunately, despite her background in&nbsp;
science and academics, Bialik isn't always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.740
viewed as a popular choice for the hosting gig&nbsp;
on "Jeopardy." This is because of controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.700
comments that she made in a New York Times&nbsp;
op-ed that many viewed as victim-blaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.820
as well as a host of other problematic&nbsp;
statements that she's made regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.260
vaccines and her partnership with the&nbsp;
controversial neurological supplement Neuriva.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.260
In response to the Harvey&nbsp;
Weinstein scandal, Bialik wrote:

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.240
"I still make choices every day as a 41-year-old&nbsp;
actress that I think of as self-protecting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.040
wise. I have decided that my sexual self is&nbsp;
best reserved for private situations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.500
those I am most intimate with. I dress modestly.&nbsp;
I don't act flirtatiously with men as a policy."

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.040
It is the last part of that statement&nbsp;
that drew the most ire for Bialik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.300
as many read it as shaming women or victim&nbsp;
blaming. Though she was clear throughout that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.720
she sees herself as a feminist and wishes&nbsp;
that the world were different for women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.000
and girls in the entertainment industry, the&nbsp;
blowback was swift from opponents of her piece.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.120
Actor Gabrielle Union tweeted that, when she&nbsp;
had been sexually assaulted while working at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:48.120
a Payless, she "had on a long tunic &amp; leggings."&nbsp;
She added, "so miss me w/ 'dress modestly' s---."

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.000
Elsewhere, Bialik served as&nbsp;
the public face for Neuriva,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.320
a supplement that Psychology Today called "snake&nbsp;
oil." This made some question her intentions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.820
as many took it as Bialik using her celebrity to&nbsp;
cash in without properly vetting the product that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.540
she was selling on CBS between episodes&nbsp;
of "Jeopardy" and "The Big Bang Theory."

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.800
This isn't all when it comes to the criticism&nbsp;
that Bialik has received in terms of her takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.180
on medical matters. Despite being a scientist&nbsp;
herself, the actor said previously that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.020
did not vaccinate her children. Again,&nbsp;
she walked this back somewhat by getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.897
the flu shot and COVID-19 vaccine later on,&nbsp;
but for many, the damage was already done.

00:02:20.897 --> 00:02:25.517
"This year, I'm going to do something&nbsp;
I literally haven't done in 30 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.065 --> 00:02:27.925
I'm gonna get a vaccine."

00:02:27.925 --> 00:02:31.320
This has been compounded by her promotion&nbsp;
of Attachment Parenting in her book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.960
"Beyond the Sling: A Real-Life&nbsp;
Guide to Raising Confident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.160
Loving Children the Attachment Parenting&nbsp;
Way." This is because Attachment Parenting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.620
or AP, is a belief system that is often seen as&nbsp;
going hand-in-hand with anti-vaccine beliefs.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.060
While some fans might point to fellow "Jeopardy"&nbsp;
host Ken Jennings as the easy alternative to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.620
Bialik in the wake of these issues, Jennings also&nbsp;
has a history of making problematic statements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.480
In particular, the host and longtime "Jeopardy"&nbsp;
contestant tweeted out an ableist post on Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.200
writing that there's, quote, "Nothing&nbsp;
sadder than a hot person in a wheelchair."

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.940
Jennings has also been accused of sexism and&nbsp;
favoritism. In an episode of "Jeopardy" that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.440
aired on September 14, 2022, Jennings&nbsp;
allowed a contestant to amend his answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.280
after he said "Constant" instead of&nbsp;
"Constable," a factor that many fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.180
found to be a double standard after he ruled&nbsp;
an answer incorrect from a previous female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.540
contestant because she mispronounced&nbsp;
the answer, despite being correct.

00:03:21.786 --> 00:03:22.506
"Oh s—!!"

00:03:22.506 --> 00:03:29.460
Sadly, this seems to show that neither of Alex&nbsp;
Trebek's replacements may be the right person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.180
for the job, especially when you consider&nbsp;
the high bar that was set by the host's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.020
squeaky-clean background and generally&nbsp;
beloved disposition. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.849
some fans think both co-hosts are, well …

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:41.547
"They're both losers."

